"use strict";(self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara=self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara||[]).push([["47218"],{13856(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"FIN-1476","slug":"average-contract-value","term":"Average Contract Value (ACV)","also_known_as":["ACV"],"difficulty":"Advanced","definition":"Average contract value (ACV) is the average annual revenue a company earns from each customer contract, used mainly by subscription and software businesses selling multi-year agreements.","explanation":["ACV normalizes contracts of different lengths into an annual figure, making them easier to compare. It is usually calculated by dividing a contract\'s total value by its length in years, excluding one-time fees, and then averaging across contracts.","For example, a three-year contract worth 90,000 in total has an ACV of 30,000. If a company\'s contracts average 30,000 a year, that is its ACV.","ACV helps companies decide how to sell. Products with low ACV often need inexpensive, self-service sales channels, while high-ACV products can justify dedicated sales teams and long sales cycles.","ACV is not an accounting measure, so definitions vary. Some companies use the term to mean annual contract value for a single contract rather than an average. Comparing ACV over time shows whether the company is winning larger deals. Investors often consider it alongside customer acquisition cost and retention."],"note":"","total":1500,"prev":{"slug":"automatic-stabilizer","term":"Automatic Stabilizer"},"next":{"slug":"average-revenue-per-user","term":"Average Revenue per User (ARPU)"}}')}}]);